1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,514
Is your paycheck different every month?

2
00:00:02,934 --> 00:00:08,008
Gig work, tips, commission, freelance. Here's how to budget when you can't predict the number.

3
00:00:08,428 --> 00:00:17,339
Our example: over the last year, this person averaged $3,200 a month. But some months were $2,200, and some were $4,400.

4
00:00:17,759 --> 00:00:24,942
These are after-tax numbers. If you're self-employed, move your tax money into its own account first, every time you get paid.

5
00:00:25,712 --> 00:00:31,104
The fix is to turn a bumpy income into a steady paycheck that you pay yourself. Four steps.

6
00:00:31,874 --> 00:00:32,794
First, the trap.

7
00:00:33,214 --> 00:00:39,919
Most people budget on their average. But spend $3,200 every month, and 5 months out of 12, you come up short.

8
00:00:40,339 --> 00:00:43,638
Those short months end up on a credit card. That's the trap.

9
00:00:44,408 --> 00:00:46,039
Step one. Find your floor.

10
00:00:46,459 --> 00:00:50,526
Your floor is your lowest month from the last year. Here, $2,200.

11
00:00:50,946 --> 00:01:00,980
Build a bare-bones budget that fits under it: rent, utilities, groceries, insurance, minimum payments. Here, $2,100. Even your worst month covers it.

12
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:03,740
Step two. Pay yourself a salary.

13
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:12,691
Every dollar you earn goes into one holding account. Then, on a set schedule, you pay yourself a fixed amount from it, like a regular paycheck.

14
00:01:13,111 --> 00:01:16,526
Pick a salary below your average. Here, $2,800 a month.

15
00:01:16,946 --> 00:01:22,337
Now the bumps happen inside the holding account, and your life runs on a steady $2,800.

16
00:01:22,757 --> 00:01:27,921
Here's the holding account, starting from zero. Good months fill it. Lean months draw it down.

17
00:01:28,341 --> 00:01:32,252
And it never runs dry. By December, there's $4,800 extra.

18
00:01:33,022 --> 00:01:35,661
Step three. Good months build the cushion first.

19
00:01:36,081 --> 00:01:43,227
Keep the extra in the holding account until it reaches one month of salary: $2,800. Here, that happens in July.

20
00:01:43,647 --> 00:01:47,920
After that, the extra goes to your goals. Debt, sinking funds, savings.

21
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:50,755
Step four. Raise your salary slowly.

22
00:01:51,175 --> 00:01:58,208
If the cushion keeps growing, give yourself a raise. But only after a full year of numbers. One great month isn't a trend.

23
00:01:58,628 --> 00:01:59,235
Recap.

24
00:01:59,395 --> 00:02:00,353
Find your floor.

25
00:02:00,513 --> 00:02:02,290
Build a bare-bones budget under it.

26
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:03,905
Pay yourself a fixed salary.

27
00:02:04,065 --> 00:02:06,212
And fill the cushion first, then your goals.

28
00:02:06,982 --> 00:02:12,921
Pay yourself twice a month, $1,400 each, and the Paycheck Planner in the description handles the rest.

29
00:02:13,341 --> 00:02:17,481
Subscribe. Next time: how big your emergency fund should really be.
